{"id":8188498,"docketId":65286227,"caseName":"Dill v. Colorado","caseNameFull":"Dill v. Colorado","slug":"dill-v-colorado","docketNumber":"No. 991","dateFiled":"1934-05-21","dateArgued":null,"dateCertGranted":null,"year":1934,"scdbId":"","direction":"","votesMajority":null,"votesMinority":null,"disposition":"","posture":"","syllabus":null,"summary":null,"headnotes":null,"proceduralHistory":null,"attorneys":"Mr. Charles Ginsberg for appellant. Mr. Paul P. Prosser for appellee.","natureOfSuit":"","citationCount":0,"judges":"","jurisdictionType":"","opinions":[{"id":8150429,"type":"majority","typeCode":"020lead","perCuriam":false,"author":"","joinedBy":"","pageCount":null,"textLength":2444,"isHtml":true,"text":"<opinion type=\"majority\">\n<author id=\"AHhq\">\n<em>Per Curiam:</em>\n</author>\n<p id=\"AYM\">The appeal herein is dismissed for the want of a substantial federal question. <em>Rosenberg </em>v. <em>Wisconsin, </em><span class=\"citation\" data-id=\"8149713\"><a href=\"/opinion/8187782/rosenberg-v-wisconsin/#601\" aria-description=\"Citation for case: Rosenberg v. Wisconsin\">290 U.S. 600, 601</a></span>; <em>Mueller </em>v. <em>Illinois, </em><span class=\"citation\" data-id=\"8149675\"><a href=\"/opinion/8187744/mueller-v-illinois/\" aria-description=\"Citation for case: Mueller v. Illinois\">289 U.S. 711</a></span>; Leach v. <em>California, </em><span class=\"citation\" data-id=\"8149128\"><a href=\"/opinion/8187198/leach-v-california/#590\" aria-description=\"Citation for case: Leach v. California\">287 U.S. 579, 590</a></span>; <em>Lavine </em>v. <em>California, </em><span class=\"citation\" data-id=\"8148930\"><a href=\"/opinion/8187000/lavine-v-california/\" aria-description=\"Citation for case: Lavine v. California\">286 U.S. 528</a></span>; <em>Sproles </em>v. <em>Binford, </em><span class=\"citation\" data-id=\"101935\"><a href=\"/opinion/101935/sproles-v-binford/#393\" aria-description=\"Citation for case: Sproles v. Binford\">286 U.S. 374, 393</a></span>; <em>Bandini </em>v. <page-number citation-index=\"1\" label=\"610\">*610</page-number><em>Superior Court, </em><span class=\"citation\" data-id=\"101792\"><a href=\"/opinion/101792/bandini-petroleum-co-v-superior-court-los-angeles-cty/#18\" aria-description=\"Citation for case: Bandini Petroleum Co. v. Superior Court, Los Angeles Cty.\">284 U.S. 8, 18</a></span>; <em>Hygrade Provision Co. </em>v. <em>Sherman, </em><span class=\"citation\" data-id=\"1620902\"><a href=\"/opinion/1620902/hygrade-provision-co-v-sherman/#501\" aria-description=\"Citation for case: Hygrade Provision Co. v. Sherman\">266 U.S. 497, 501-503</a></span>. Insofar as the papers whereon the appeal was allowed seek review in respect of asserted denial of rights under the Federal Constitution by rulings of the Supreme Court of Colorado not involving the validity of any statute of the state, such papers are treated as a petition for writ of certiorari (\u00a7 237 (c), Judicial Code, as amended by the Act of February 13, 1925, <span class=\"citation no-link\">43 Stat. 936</span>, 938), and certiorari is denied.</p>\n<attorneys id=\"ASXk\"><em>Mr. Charles Ginsberg </em>for appellant. <em>Mr. Paul P. Prosser </em>for appellee.</attorneys>\n</opinion>"}],"citations":[]}